(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-8-hematologic-malignancies-ii-vignettes"]={"title":"Hematologic Malignancies II Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 8","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 8","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has an absolute lymphocyte count of 18,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) on two blood counts 3 months apart. She feels well, with no lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. Which test confirms the suspected diagnosis?","choices":["Flow cytometry","Bone marrow biopsy","Excisional node biopsy","Protein electrophoresis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her persistent lymphocytosis above 5,000 suggests chronic lymphocytic leukemia, which is confirmed by flow cytometry identifying a clonal B-cell population (coexpressing CD19 (cluster of differentiation 19) and CD5 (cluster of differentiation 5)). It usually does not need a bone marrow biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman being treated for laboratory-confirmed pertussis has an absolute lymphocyte count of 7,900 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) with atypical lymphocytes on the smear. She has no lymphadenopathy or organomegaly. What is the best interpretation of her lymphocytosis?","choices":["Reactive infectious lymphocytosis","Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Burkitt lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atypical lymphocytosis can be seen with some infections, including pertussis, which is on the differential of chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Her proven infection, young age (that leukemia has a median age of 70) and clean examination fit a reactive cause. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man with Burkitt lymphoma started multi-agent chemotherapy 36 hours ago. His potassium is 6.6 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0 mEq/L), phosphorus 7.8 mg/dL (normal 2.5-4.5 mg/dL), uric acid 13.1 mg/dL (normal 3.5-7.2 mg/dL) and creatinine 2.4 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL). Which complication has developed?","choices":["Richter transformation","Hypercalcemia of myeloma","IgM hyperviscosity","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tumor lysis syndrome is rapid tumor breakdown releasing potassium, phosphorus and uric acid; uric acid and calcium phosphate crystals in the renal tubules cause acute renal failure. It is a medical emergency, expected after treatment of aggressive lymphoma such as Burkitt. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man has classical Hodgkin lymphoma with cervical nodes and a bulky mediastinal mass, all above the diaphragm, and drenching night sweats. Which treatment approach is appropriate?","choices":["Radiation alone","Observation","Chemotherapy plus radiation","A short course of chemotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. His nodes are on one side of the diaphragm (stage II), and the disease is bulky with B symptoms (night sweats). Bulky stage I or II disease, or disease with B symptoms, is treated with chemotherapy plus radiation. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 16, 27, 41"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with multiple myeloma has responded to initial 3-drug therapy and is a transplant candidate. Which plan has been shown to improve overall survival?","choices":["Stop treatment once in remission","Radiation to the spine alone","Watch and wait for relapse","Autologous transplant, then maintenance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a transplant candidate, autologous transplant has been shown to improve overall survival, as has maintenance therapy after transplant. Most patients still relapse and need continuous therapy for disease control. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man has back pain and constipation. His M-protein is 4.6 g/dL and bone marrow biopsy shows 38% clonal plasma cells. Calcium is 12.1 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL), hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL (normal 13.5-17.5 g/dL), creatinine 2.0 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.2 mg/dL), and imaging shows lytic lesions in the spine. How should his plasma cell disorder be classified?","choices":["Reactive hypergammaglobulinemia of cirrhosis","Multiple myeloma","Smoldering multiple myeloma","Monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Myeloma is diagnosed by at least 10% marrow plasma cells (38%) plus at least one CRAB (calcium, renal, anemia, bone) feature; he has all four: hypercalcemia (12.1), renal insufficiency (creatinine 2.0), anemia (9.2) and lytic bone lesions. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 47, 50, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has newly diagnosed multiple myeloma. Her beta-2 microglobulin is 6.8 mg/L (normal 0.8-2.2 mg/L), albumin 3.9 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL), chromosomal studies show deletion 17p, and lactate dehydrogenase is above the upper limit of normal. What is her R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. R-ISS (Revised International Staging System) stage III: beta-2 microglobulin at least 5.5 mg/L regardless of albumin (6.8), high-risk chromosomal abnormalities (deletion 17p), and high lactate dehydrogenase. She meets all three, so her normal albumin does not matter. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has a painless, persistent 3-cm cervical lymph node, and Hodgkin lymphoma is suspected. The team is planning how to sample it. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Complete blood count","Fine-needle aspiration","Excisional lymph node biopsy","Chest x-ray"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fine-needle aspiration should be avoided: excisional biopsy is required to observe lymph node architecture, and aspiration may miss the diagnosis. Hodgkin lymphoma is diagnosed by excisional lymph node biopsy. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy from sub-Saharan Africa has a painless jaw swelling that his mother says grew visibly over a single day. He has fever, night sweats and weight loss, and the tumor cells test positive for Epstein-Barr virus. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Burkitt lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma","Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Burkitt lymphoma is a very aggressive lymphoma with very rapidly growing, often painless swelling (changes visible within a day) and B symptoms (fever, night sweats, weight loss). The endemic type is seen in sub-Saharan Africa and strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 31, 32"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has cutaneous T-cell lymphoma that has progressed to Sezary syndrome: a widespread, red, itchy rash (erythroderma) with severe pruritus. Staging finds no visceral involvement. What is the focus of his treatment?","choices":["Treating the skin disease","Rituximab-based chemotherapy","Antifungal therapy alone","Splenectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma (mycosis fungoides and its aggressive form, Sezary syndrome) is focused on the cutaneous manifestations, as there is often no visceral involvement. His erythroderma and pruritus diminish his quality of life. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 22, 27"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with splenomegaly is found to have splenic marginal zone lymphoma. Which associated infection should be tested for?","choices":["Epstein-Barr virus","Lyme disease","Hepatitis C virus","Helicobacter pylori"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Splenic marginal zone lymphoma is associated with hepatitis C; marginal zone lymphomas are more common in older adults. Her splenic subtype points to hepatitis C rather than Helicobacter pylori. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 17, 23"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had chronic lymphocytic leukemia, observed without treatment, for 6 years. Over 3 weeks one cervical lymph node has grown rapidly, and he now has fevers, night sweats and weight loss. Which complication has most likely occurred?","choices":["Monoclonal B lymphocytosis","Smoldering myeloma","Richter transformation","Tumor lysis syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Richter transformation is chronic lymphocytic leukemia transforming into an aggressive B-cell lymphoma, with poor outcomes. A long, stable course that suddenly turns into a rapidly growing node with B symptoms is that picture. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 28, 57"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has back pain, a hemoglobin of 9.8 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and a calcium of 11.4 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5 mg/dL). Multiple myeloma is suspected. Which blood test shows the characteristic finding?","choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase","Uric acid level","Serum protein electrophoresis","Flow cytometry of blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A monoclonal protein on serum protein electrophoresis is characteristic of myeloma, with a positive urine monoclonal protein (Bence Jones protein) on 24-hour urine electrophoresis. Work-up adds light chain ratio, bone marrow biopsy with flow, and imaging. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has chronic lymphocytic leukemia confirmed by flow cytometry. She asks whether any other test will help predict her course and guide her therapy. Which test serves that purpose?","choices":["Positron emission tomography","Urine protein electrophoresis","Cytogenetics","Bone marrow biopsy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cytogenetics is important for prognosis and has some treatment considerations. About 80% of cases carry one of four chromosomal abnormalities: deletion 13q, deletion 11q, trisomy 12 or deletion 17p. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 5, 8"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man's primary care clinician finds hypercalcemia, anemia and an M-protein, and bone marrow biopsy confirms multiple myeloma. Which referral is most appropriate?","choices":["Nephrology for kidney evaluation alone","Orthopedic surgery for the bone lesions","Endocrinology for the high calcium","Hematology/oncology, ideally a myeloma expert"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiple myeloma requires referral to and management by a hematologist/oncologist, ideally a myeloma expert, with lifelong follow-up for lab monitoring and treatment. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has nodal marginal zone lymphoma, a low-grade B-cell lymphoma, involving several node regions above the diaphragm. She has developed bothersome symptoms and now needs treatment, but her disease is not extensive. Which agent is used?","choices":["Rituximab","Cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, vincristine, prednisone","Doxorubicin, bleomycin, vinblastine, dacarbazine","Allopurinol alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-grade B-cell lymphomas are treated with rituximab (an anti-CD20 (cluster of differentiation 20) monoclonal antibody) when treatment is required, with chemotherapy added if the disease is extensive. Hers needs treatment but is not extensive. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 41"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has a 3-cm firm, painless left axillary lymph node that has grown over 6 weeks. There is no infection to explain it. Which test establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase level","Excisional lymph node biopsy","Fine-needle aspiration","Flow cytometry of blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lymphoma is diagnosed by excisional lymph node biopsy, reserved for significant enlargement (2 cm), persistent and progressively enlarging nodes; his 3-cm growing node qualifies. Excision shows the node architecture needed for the diagnosis. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 8, 25"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with chronic dyspepsia has an endoscopic gastric biopsy showing extranodal marginal zone lymphoma of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue. Which associated infection should be tested for?","choices":["Hepatitis C virus","Epstein-Barr virus","Human T-lymphotropic virus 1","Helicobacter pylori"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastric mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma, the most common extranodal marginal zone subtype, is associated with Helicobacter pylori in about two-thirds of cases. A stomach lymphoma of this type calls for testing for it. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 17, 23"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has excisional biopsy-proven diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Computed tomography and positron emission tomography show disease confined to one group of right cervical lymph nodes, with no other nodes or organs involved. What is her stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lugano staging, a modified Ann Arbor system: stage 1 is localized disease in a single lymph node region or a single organ. One right cervical node region with nothing else involved is stage 1. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with chronic lymphocytic leukemia confirmed by flow cytometry has an absolute lymphocyte count of 22,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000). She has painless, firm, mobile cervical and axillary lymph nodes; the liver and spleen are not enlarged. Hemoglobin is 13.1 g/dL (normal 12.0-15.5 g/dL) and platelets 210,000 per microliter (normal 150,000-450,000). What is her Rai stage?","choices":["Stage 0","Stage I","Stage II","Stage III"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: lymphocytosis yes (22,000); lymphadenopathy yes (cervical and axillary nodes); organomegaly no; anemia no (13.1 g/dL); thrombocytopenia no (210,000). Lymphocytosis with lymphadenopathy and nothing more is Rai stage I. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy has Burkitt lymphoma that was identified early, and he is starting multi-agent chemotherapy promptly. His parents ask about his long-term outlook. Which long-term survival figure applies to children treated this way?","choices":["Under 10%","About 25%","50-90%","Above 90%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With early identification and multi-agent chemotherapy, Burkitt lymphoma has an excellent long-term prognosis: above 90% in children and 50-90% in adults. Prompt treatment, with care for tumor lysis, is what earns that outcome. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has biopsy-proven non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Staging imaging shows involved lymph nodes in the left cervical region and the left axillary region; there are no nodes below the diaphragm and no organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Reading the node map: two node regions (cervical and axillary), both above the diaphragm, with no organ involvement. Two or more lymph node regions on the same side of the diaphragm is stage 2. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has a painless, rubbery left cervical lymph node that aches after she drinks alcohol, and intense itching for 2 months. Chest x-ray shows a mediastinal mass. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic lymphocytic leukemia","Burkitt lymphoma","Follicular lymphoma","Hodgkin lymphoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma has a bimodal incidence with a peak in adults in their 20s and presents with painless lymphadenopathy, usually cervical, that may hurt with alcohol, intense pruritus and a mediastinal mass on chest x-ray. Her picture matches item by item. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 37, 38"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma. Staging imaging shows enlarged right cervical lymph nodes and left inguinal lymph nodes, with no liver, bone marrow or other organ involvement. What is his stage?","choices":["Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 3","Stage 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cervical nodes sit above the diaphragm and inguinal nodes below it, with no organ involvement. Two or more lymph node regions above and below the diaphragm is stage 3. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has diffuse large B-cell lymphoma involving cervical lymph nodes, the liver and the bone marrow. Her ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) performance status is 1, and her lactate dehydrogenase is normal. How many poor-prognosis factors does she have?","choices":["One factor","Two factors","Three factors","Four factors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Item by item: age over 60, no (52); extranodal sites, yes (liver and marrow); ECOG (Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group) status above 2, no (1); stage III or IV, yes (multiple organs is stage 4); elevated lactate dehydrogenase, no. Total 2. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 26, 30"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has classical Hodgkin lymphoma of the mixed cellularity subtype, stage II and not bulky. Which features of her case predict inferior results?","choices":["Her age and bulky disease","Her age and subtype","Her sex and stage","Her stage and subtype"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hodgkin lymphoma is generally curable, but inferior results are seen in older patients, those with bulky disease and those with certain subtypes (lymphocyte depleted or mixed cellularity). She is older and has mixed cellularity; her disease is not bulky. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 41, 42"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has monoclonal B lymphocytosis: an absolute lymphocyte count of 4,300 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) with a clonal B-cell population and no lymphadenopathy, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms. He asks how often this progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia. Which rate is accurate?","choices":["About 1-2% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Monoclonal B lymphocytosis progresses to chronic lymphocytic leukemia at a rate of about 1-2% per year. His count below 5,000 with no nodes, organomegaly, cytopenias or symptoms keeps him in the precursor state for now. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 9, 57"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has a non-aggressive (low-grade) B-cell lymphoma confined to a single lymph node region in the left groin; staging shows no other disease. Which treatment fits this presentation?","choices":["Ibrutinib","Allogeneic transplant","Radiation therapy","Multi-drug chemotherapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Management depends on subtype and presentation: a single node of non-aggressive disease is treated with radiation. His staging shows one region only (stage 1) of low-grade disease. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 27, 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance: M-protein 1.1 g/dL, 3% clonal bone marrow plasma cells and no myeloma-defining characteristics. She asks how likely it is to become myeloma. Which rate is accurate?","choices":["About 1% per year","About 10% per year","About 25% per year","About 50% per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risk of progression from monoclonal gammopathy of undetermined significance to myeloma is about 1% per year. Her low M-protein, under 10% plasma cells and lack of myeloma-defining features keep her in this low-risk precursor stage. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has chronic lymphocytic leukemia, Rai stage 0, found on routine blood work. He has no symptoms, and his absolute lymphocyte count of 12,000 per microliter (normal 1,000-4,000) has been stable for 9 months. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Start ibrutinib now","Venetoclax plus rituximab","Excision of the nodes","Observation (watch and wait)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic patients may be observed (watch and wait) until there is rapid progression (lymphocyte count doubling in under 6 months) or disease-related symptoms. Treatment begins with symptomatic disease or rapid progression; he has neither. — 50. Hematologic Malignancies Part II - Gannon.pptx, Slides 10, 11"}]};
